concepts.body:
A **trait** names a set of methods that many types can provide. Each type opts in with an `impl` block:

```rust
trait Ship {
    fn name(&self) -> &str;          // required: every ship must say this
    fn hail(&self) -> String {       // default: free for every implementor
        format!("Ahoy from {}!", self.name())
    }
}

struct Sloop { name: String }

impl Ship for Sloop {
    fn name(&self) -> &str { &self.name }
    // hail() is inherited from the default
}
```

A **default method** may call the required methods. An implementor can **override** it by writing its own version. Put shared logic in the trait once instead of copying it into every `impl`.

A `Vec<T>` holds one type. A mixed fleet needs **trait objects**:

```rust
let fleet: Vec<Box<dyn Ship>> = vec![
    Box::new(Sloop { name: "Swift".into() }),
    Box::new(Galleon { name: "Goldtooth".into() }),
];
for ship in &fleet {
    println!("{}", ship.hail()); // which hail()? decided at runtime
}
```

A `Box<dyn Ship>` (or `&dyn Ship`) is a **fat pointer**: two words. One points at the data, and the other points at a **vtable**, a static table holding the type's size, its drop function and one function pointer per trait method. Calling `ship.hail()` looks up the slot in the vtable and jumps there.

